<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Bilateral gynecomastia from exogenous estrogen (N62)</content>. Typical bilateral gynecomastia on examination, so breast imaging is not needed. Endocrinology agrees that the estrogen explains it: hCG and AFP are normal and the scrotal ultrasound shows no mass. Estradiol is falling (46 pg/mL). [S]<list><item>Watchful waiting; no tamoxifen or other drug, as Dr. Montrose-Adeyemi advised.</item><item>A compression shirt for running if he wishes.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Drug-induced hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (E23.1)</content>. LH still suppressed at 0.9 mIU/mL five weeks after the last injection; recovery is expected over two to six months. [S]<list><item>No testosterone therapy while the axis recovers.</item><item>Testosterone, estradiol, LH, FSH, prolactin, and hemoglobin in six weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, moderate (F32.1)</content>. PHQ-9 14, down from 15, two weeks into psychotherapy. No past mania or hypomania. Suicide risk remains low, with no ideation since intake and protective factors unchanged. [S]<list><item>Continue weekly psychotherapy; review the antidepressant question at eight weeks if the PHQ-9 has not fallen by half.</item><item>Collaborative safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the clinic number confirmed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Breast examination for size, tenderness, and any discrete mass</td><td>Every visit until the gynecomastia settles</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Firm, tender glandular tissue about 4 cm behind each nipple; symmetric; no discrete mass, skin change, discharge, or nodes</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Breast ultrasound or mammography</td><td>Only if the examination is equivocal or a discrete mass is felt</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Typical bilateral, centered gynecomastia on examination</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Scrotal ultrasound</td><td>Once, in the gynecomastia workup</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Testes about 15 mL each, homogeneous; no mass; no varicocele</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Estradiol and LH while the estrogen clears</td><td>Every 6 to 8 weeks after the exposure stops, until normal</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Estradiol 46 pg/mL, down from 148; LH 0.9 mIU/mL, still suppressed</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>hCG, AFP, liver, kidney, and thyroid tests</td><td>Once, at the initial workup</td><td>Up to date</td><td>All normal at intake two weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Testosterone therapy</td><td>Only for persistent, symptomatic, confirmed testosterone deficiency</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not given while the axis recovers: it would suppress LH and FSH again and is not indicated for a transient, drug-induced low testosterone</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Endocrinology consultation</td><td>For gynecomastia with abnormal hormone results</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; drug-induced cause confirmed; recovery monitoring agreed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline, then at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>14, down from 15 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, or behavior since intake; PHQ-9 item 9 scored 0. Protective factors unchanged. Safety plan reviewed: on his phone, with 988 and the clinic number</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Bipolar screen at the initial workup</td><td>Once, at the initial depression workup</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Interview found no past manic or hypomanic episode and no family history known to him; no Mood Disorder Questionnaire score is recorded</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Psychotherapy, sleep, running, and social contact</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two sessions with a gay-affirming psychologist; sleep five to six hours; two runs alone</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lithium, anticonvulsant, or antipsychotic monitoring (levels, metabolic tests, AIMS)</td><td>Only on those drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not on any of these drugs</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Testosterone, LH, and FSH back in the reference range within six months of the last estrogen dose, without testosterone therapy. In progress; target 2026-08-04 [S]</item><item>Breast tenderness gone and gynecomastia reduced; a surgical opinion offered if it persists and troubles him. In progress; target 2026-10-06 [S]</item><item>Depression response (PHQ-9 down by at least 50 percent) by sixteen weeks, then remission (PHQ-9 below 5). In progress; target 2026-10-06 [S]</item><item>Back to running about 40 km a week and changing at his club again. In progress; target 2026-10-06 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: EAA clinical practice guidelines: gynecomastia evaluation and management (2019); Testosterone Therapy in Men With Hypogonadism: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2018); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in six weeks with hormone tests and PHQ-9. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-09: eight-week review of hormone recovery and depression treatment. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
